  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1044 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nSunday evening satellite and upper air observations place a\nclosed upper trough over the New Jersey and New York Coastline.\nTo its west, weak northerlies prevail over the Mississippi River\nValley as far south as the Gulf Coast with a weak axis of ridging\nalong the front range of the Rockies. Surface observations show a\nvery dry air mass present over the Mid-South with dewpoints\nranging between 45 - 55 F. Such dry air will lead to quick\nchanges in temperature, which is expected to allow temperatures\nto fall into the low 60s and 50s tonight. Furthermore, cloud\ncover, which was forecast to be more prevalent by this point in\nthe forecast, has generally failed to develop, leading to a\nfaster drop in temperatures. Therefore, some areas could see lows\napproach 50 degrees Monday morning, particularly across portions\nof West Tennessee near the Tennessee River.\n\nSimilar conditions are expected Monday to that of Sunday with a\ndry, seasonable air mass in place. Expect highs in the 70s and\n80s and precipitation-free weather. However, both the REFS and\nHREF show afternoon relative humidity values dropping well below\n30%, leading to elevated fire weather conditions. Portions of the\nregion, particularly across northern Mississippi, have a 50%\nchance of relative humidity values below 20%, which much of the\nregion achieved today. This same forecast, or something similar,\nlooks likely on Tuesday as well given the upper and surface\npatterns are expected to remain persistent. So, expect elevated\nfire danger to persist through Tuesday.\n\nBy Wednesday, what the remnants of Hurricane Polo will have\nphased into the eastern side of an upper trough crossing the\nsouthern Rockies. The combined trough axis will then swing into\nthe southern Plains through Wednesday and Thursday with ridging\namplifying upstream of the troughing over the southeastern CONUS.\nIn response, southerlies are forecast to return to the Mid-South,\nleading to an increase in temperatures beginning Tuesday and\nincreasing humidity Wednesday which will linger Thursday.\nMeanwhile, ensemble guidance continues to encourage higher\nforecast confidence for the end of the week with an increase in\nprecipitation chances late Thursday and into next weekend.\nHowever, there are still notable differences in the evolution of\nthe surface pattern amongst guidance that will dictate the\noverall scope and magnitude of any precipitation into next\nweekend. Current NBM guidance has roughly a 40% - 50% chance for\n12 hour rainfall accumulation to exceed .25\" Saturday and Sunday.\nSo, rainier weather may be on the horizon by next weekend, but\nforecast uncertainties will likely allow for changes to the\nprecipitation forecast through this week.",
